<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 3: Exploring Data                                        -->
<!--                                                                    -->
<!--  Data-first chapter. Deliberately light: notice patterns, ask      -->
<!--  questions, read a pivot table. No formal definitions of mean,     -->
<!--  variance, missingness taxonomy, etc. - those are introduced only  -->
<!--  when they are needed, in later chapters:                         -->
<!--    - the "why is this blank" discussion    -> Cleaning Data        -->
<!--    - coloured / faceted / relational plots -> Visualising          -->
<!--      Multidimensional Data                                        -->
<!--    - factors, grouping mechanics           -> Handling Factors     -->
<!--    - MCAR/MAR/MNAR, deletion vs imputation -> Handling Missing     -->
<!--      Values                                                       -->
<!--    - formal mean/median/variance/SD/IQR    -> Measuring and        -->
<!--      Observing Variability                                        -->
<!--  This chapter's job is only to make the reader curious enough to   -->
<!--  want those later chapters.                                       -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-exploring-data" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Exploring Data</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-exploring-data">
    <p>
      <xref ref="ch-intro" /> got you comfortable clicking around in R and
      RStudio, and the module's earlier look at this dataset introduced the
      idea that it has a structure to it: individual birds, grouped within
      lakes. Neither of those chapters asked you to draw a conclusion from the
      data. This one does.
    </p>

    <p>
      There is no formula to learn in this chapter, and nothing here is the
      <q>correct</q> way to analyse anything. The goal is smaller and more
      important than that: open the data, look at it, and let it surprise you.
      Every method introduced later in this module <mdash /> cleaning,
      multidimensional graphics, factors, formal treatment of missing values,
      measures of variability <mdash /> exists to answer a question that this
      chapter is going to raise and deliberately leave open.
    </p>

    <note xml:id="note-flamingo-dataset">
      <title>The dataset used throughout this module</title>
      <p>
        Most examples in this module use <c>kenya_flamingos.csv</c>, which
        records measurements on 344 flamingos caught, measured and released
        across three Rift Valley soda lakes.
      </p>
      <p>
        This dataset is <em>synthetic</em>. The differences between the two
        species in size and bill shape are grounded in real flamingo biology,
        and the lake compositions reflect documented patterns, but the
        individual measurements were generated rather than observed. It exists
        for teaching. It should not be cited as evidence about real flamingo
        populations.
      </p>
    </note>
  </introduction>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 1: the data-first hook                                   -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-flamingo-hook">
    <title>A Question About Flamingos</title>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-three-lakes-two-species">
      <title>Three lakes, two species, 344 birds</title>

      <p>TODO: Need to do a new example here. Something better! With multiple levels!</p>
      <p>TODO: Flamingoes can be another example in here, like a STACK type question </p>

      <p>
        Kenya's Rift Valley holds a chain of shallow, strongly alkaline soda
        lakes. The alkalinity is hostile to most life but ideal for the
        cyanobacteria and diatoms that flamingos filter-feed on, which is why
        these lakes support some of the largest flamingo congregations
        anywhere.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two species share them. The <term>lesser flamingo</term> is the
        smaller of the two, with a deep, sharply decurved bill adapted to
        filtering cyanobacteria from the water column. The <term>greater
        flamingo</term> is considerably larger, with a longer and shallower
        bill, and feeds more on invertebrates in the sediment.
      </p>

      <p>
        A field team has measured 344 birds across Lakes Nakuru, Bogoria and
        Elementaita. Each row of the dataset describes one bird.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-flamingo-variables">
        <title>Variables recorded for each bird in <c>kenya_flamingos.csv</c></title>
        <tabular halign="left">
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Variable</cell>
            <cell>Type</cell>
            <cell>Description</cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell><c>lake</c></cell><cell>categorical</cell><cell>Nakuru, Bogoria or Elementaita</cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>species</c></cell><cell>categorical</cell><cell>Greater Flamingo or Lesser Flamingo</cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>bill_length_mm</c></cell><cell>numeric</cell><cell>Bill length in millimetres</cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>bill_depth_mm</c></cell><cell>numeric</cell><cell>Bill depth at the base, in millimetres</cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>leg_length_mm</c></cell><cell>numeric</cell><cell>Tarsus length in millimetres</cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>body_mass_g</c></cell><cell>numeric</cell><cell>Body mass in grams</cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>sex</c></cell><cell>categorical</cell><cell>male or female</cell></row>
          <row><cell><c>year</c></cell><cell>numeric</cell><cell>Year of measurement (2021<ndash />2023)</cell></row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Load the file and look at the first few rows. Do this yourself before
        reading on <mdash /> what do you notice, just from six rows?
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        flamingos &lt;- read.csv("kenya_flamingos.csv", stringsAsFactors = TRUE)
        head(flamingos)

        #&gt;          lake          species bill_length_mm bill_depth_mm leg_length_mm
        #&gt; 1     Bogoria  Lesser Flamingo          101.2          25.9         265.1
        #&gt; 2      Nakuru Greater Flamingo          135.8          22.5         371.8
        #&gt; 3      Nakuru  Lesser Flamingo           96.1          26.9         278.0
        #&gt; 4 Elementaita Greater Flamingo          131.1          25.7         360.4
        #&gt; 5      Nakuru  Lesser Flamingo           94.1          24.3         264.1
        #&gt; 6      Nakuru  Lesser Flamingo           98.4          25.9         272.3
        #&gt;   body_mass_g    sex year
        #&gt; 1        1330 female 2022
        #&gt; 2        2841 female 2023
        #&gt; 3        1646   male 2022
        #&gt; 4        3476   male 2022
        #&gt; 5        1620 female 2021
        #&gt; 6        1963   male 2022
      </program>

      <p>
        Even in six rows, the greater flamingos already look heavier and
        longer-billed than the lesser flamingos. Six rows is manageable to
        scan by eye. All 344 is not <mdash /> which is exactly the problem
        this chapter is about: how do you get a feel for a dataset you cannot
        just read?
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-first-obvious-question">
      <title>A first, obvious question</title>

      <p>
        <em>How big is a flamingo in the Rift Valley?</em>
      </p>

      <p>
        This is the sort of question a conservation officer or a field
        ecologist might genuinely ask. The instinctive move is to add up the
        masses of every bird and divide by how many birds there are <mdash />
        the <term>average</term>.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        mean(flamingos$body_mass_g)
        #&gt; [1] NA
      </program>

      <p>
        Not what we expected. R has returned <c>NA</c>, <q>not available,</q>
        because some birds in the dataset have no recorded mass and R refuses
        to quietly pretend otherwise. Why some birds are missing a mass, and
        what we are allowed to do about it, is a real question <mdash /> and
        it is the subject of a later chapter, <term>Cleaning Data</term>, in
        full. For now we just want an answer, so we tell R to set the missing
        values aside.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        mean(flamingos$body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 1957.405
      </program>

      <p>
        So the average Rift Valley flamingo weighs about 1957 g, just under
        two kilograms. That is our answer, and the arithmetic behind it is
        correct.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-something-is-wrong">
      <title>Something is wrong</title>

      <p>
        Before moving on, sanity-check the answer. How many of the birds
        actually weigh close to 1957 g? Let's ask something more specific:
        how many weigh <em>less</em> than that.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        mass &lt;- flamingos$body_mass_g

        sum(mass &lt; 1957.405, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 253

        sum(!is.na(mass))    # birds that actually have a recorded mass
        #&gt; [1] 336
      </program>

      <observation xml:id="obs-three-quarters">
        <title>The problem</title>
        <p>
          253 of the 336 measured birds, <em>75.3%</em>, weigh less than the
          average bird. Three quarters of the flamingos are lighter than the
          average flamingo.
        </p>
      </observation>

      <p>
        Pause on that before reading on. Nothing has gone wrong with the
        arithmetic <mdash /> <c>mean()</c> computed exactly what it is defined
        to compute. So what has gone wrong?
      </p>

      <p>
        The instinct at this point is to reach for a different summary,
        perhaps the <term>median</term> (sort the values and take the middle
        one) is <q>more correct.</q> Let's try it.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        median(mass, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 1758.5
      </program>

      <p>
        1758.5 g, which is 199 g lower than the mean <mdash /> about 11%
        below it. That is a substantial disagreement between two numbers that
        both claim to describe the same thing. Swapping one summary for
        another hasn't resolved anything; it has produced a second answer with
        no obvious reason to prefer it over the first.
      </p>

      <p>
        The problem is not which summary we picked. <em>The problem is the
        question.</em>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-shape-of-the-data">
      <title>Looking at the shape of the data</title>

      <p>
        Rather than computing yet another single number, let's look at how
        the masses are actually distributed. Group them into 500 g bands,
        labelled in kilograms, and count how many birds fall in each.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        # Group into 500 g bands, labelled in kilograms
        bands &lt;- cut(mass, breaks = seq(1000, 4000, by = 500),
                     labels = c("1.0-1.5", "1.5-2.0", "2.0-2.5",
                                "2.5-3.0", "3.0-3.5", "3.5-4.0"))
        table(bands)

        #&gt; bands
        #&gt; 1.0-1.5 1.5-2.0 2.0-2.5 2.5-3.0 3.0-3.5 3.5-4.0
        #&gt;      50     209      15      20      34       8
      </program>

      <p>
        Read the counts across: 50, then 209, then a collapse to
        <term>15</term>, then back up to 20, 34 and 8. The same picture is
        easier to take in as a histogram.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-mass-histogram">
        <caption>
          Body mass (g) of 336 captured flamingos, showing two separate
          concentrations of mass rather than one.
        </caption>
        <image source="images/flamingo-mass-histogram.png" width="60%"/>
        <description>
          A histogram of body mass with two separated peaks: a tall one
          around 1.5-2.0 kg and a shorter one around 3.0-3.5 kg, with very
          few birds in between.
        </description>
      </figure>

      <p>
        That dip in the middle is not noise. The data has two separate
        concentrations of mass <mdash /> a large one below 2000 g and a
        smaller one above 2500 g <mdash /> with a near-empty valley between
        them. A distribution with two peaks like this is called
        <term>bimodal</term><idx><h>bimodal</h></idx>, and it is precisely
        where our average landed: 1957 g sits on the shoulder of the lower
        group, pointing into the gap.
      </p>

      <p>
        The mean is the balance point of the data. When the data has two
        clusters, the balance point falls between them, describing neither.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-splitting-by-species">
      <title>The resolution</title>

      <p>
        We already know something that might explain this: there are two
        species in the dataset. Split the masses by species and look at each
        group on its own.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        table(flamingos$species)

        #&gt; Greater Flamingo  Lesser Flamingo
        #&gt;               66              278

        tapply(mass, flamingos$species, mean, na.rm = TRUE)

        #&gt; Greater Flamingo  Lesser Flamingo
        #&gt;         3120.190         1689.070
      </program>

      <p>
        There is the answer. Greater flamingos average about 3120 g; lesser
        flamingos about 1689 g. These are not slightly different populations
        <mdash /> the greater flamingo is nearly twice the mass of the lesser.
        Because lesser flamingos outnumber greaters roughly four to one, the
        pooled average is dragged toward the lesser group but not all the way,
        landing at 1957 g <mdash /> a value that describes no bird in this
        dataset in any useful sense.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>It is heavier than 91% of the lesser flamingos.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            It is lighter than <em>every single</em> greater flamingo. The
            lightest greater in the dataset is 2347 g.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <insight xml:id="insight-what-went-wrong">
        <title>What actually went wrong</title>
        <p>
          The question <q>how big is a flamingo?</q> presupposes that there is
          one kind of flamingo. The dataset contains two.
        </p>
        <p>
          No choice of summary function could have fixed this on its own,
          because the error was not in the arithmetic. It lay in applying a
          single-population summary to a two-population dataset. The mean
          answered faithfully; we asked badly. Noticing that <mdash /> before
          trusting a single number <mdash /> is what this chapter is about.
        </p>
      </insight>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-what-next">
      <title>What this chapter has, and hasn't, done</title>

      <p>
        Nothing in this section required a formula. Loading the data, asking
        a plain-language question, computing an obvious average, and noticing
        that it didn't add up <mdash /> that was enough to uncover real
        structure in the data. That is the whole method of this chapter: look
        first, and let what you see tell you what to ask next.
      </p>

      <p>
        It has also deliberately left several things unresolved. We waved a
        hand at the missing masses. We used <q>mean</q> and <q>median</q>
        without defining them carefully, or asking how much a group's spread
        matters, or what happens when we compare groups of very different
        sizes. We looked at one histogram and one table, not the many other
        ways this data could be pictured. Each of those loose ends is picked
        up properly later in the module.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-roadmap-exploring">
        <title>Loose ends from this chapter, and where they get resolved</title>
        <tabular halign="left">
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>What we glossed over here</cell>
            <cell>Picked up properly in</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Why some birds have no recorded mass, and whether it's safe to ignore them</cell>
            <cell><term>Cleaning Data</term></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Colouring, faceting, and otherwise plotting more than one variable at once</cell>
            <cell><term>Visualising Multidimensional Data</term></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>What <c>species</c>, <c>lake</c> and <c>sex</c> actually are to R, and how grouping works mechanically</cell>
            <cell><term>Handling Factors</term></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Formal rules for when dropping missing values is, and isn't, safe</cell>
            <cell><term>Handling Missing Values</term></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>What the mean and median actually are, and how to measure spread rather than eyeballing it</cell>
            <cell><term>Measuring and Observing Variability</term></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 2: pivot tables                                          -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-pivot-tables">
    <title>Pivot Tables: Where Do the Birds Live?</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Body mass is only one variable. The dataset also records which lake
        each bird was caught at. Do the two species mix freely across all
        three lakes, or does each lake favour one species over the other? A
        <term>pivot table</term><idx><h>pivot table</h></idx> <mdash />
        counting how often combinations of two categorical variables occur
        together <mdash /> is the natural first tool for this, whether you
        build it in R, in a spreadsheet, or by hand from a small enough
        dataset.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-a-simple-pivot-table">
      <title>Counting lake against species</title>

      <p>
        In R, the same function that gave us a one-way count of species gives
        a two-way count when handed two variables.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        table(flamingos$lake, flamingos$species)

        #&gt;               Greater Flamingo  Lesser Flamingo
        #&gt;   Bogoria                   10              114
        #&gt;   Elementaita               30               38
        #&gt;   Nakuru                    26              126
      </program>

      <table xml:id="table-lake-species-crosstab">
        <title>Flamingo captures by lake and species</title>
        <tabular halign="left">
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Lake</cell>
            <cell>Greater Flamingo</cell>
            <cell>Lesser Flamingo</cell>
            <cell>Total</cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell>Bogoria</cell><cell>10</cell><cell>114</cell><cell>124</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Elementaita</cell><cell>30</cell><cell>38</cell><cell>68</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Nakuru</cell><cell>26</cell><cell>126</cell><cell>152</cell></row>
          <row><cell>Total</cell><cell>66</cell><cell>278</cell><cell>344</cell></row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Before reading on: which lake has caught the most lesser flamingos, in
        raw numbers? Now ask a different question <mdash /> at which lake do
        lesser flamingos make up the <em>largest share</em> of the birds
        caught there? Look at the table again. Are those the same lake?
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-row-column-proportions">
      <title>Two different questions, two different proportions</title>

      <p>
        Nakuru has the most lesser flamingos in absolute terms, 126, simply
        because Nakuru is the biggest sample of the three lakes. But relative
        to each lake's own total, Bogoria is where lesser flamingos dominate
        most heavily:
      </p>

      <me>\frac{114}{124} \approx 0.919, \quad \text{compared with} \quad \frac{126}{152} \approx 0.829 \text{ at Nakuru}</me>

      <p>
        These are two genuinely different questions, and mixing them up is
        one of the easiest mistakes to make when reading a pivot table.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <q>Of the birds at Bogoria, what fraction are lesser flamingos?</q>
            divides each row by its own row total.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <q>Of all lesser flamingos, what fraction were caught at
            Bogoria?</q> divides down each column instead.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <program language="r">
        # row proportions: within each lake, split by species
        prop.table(table(flamingos$lake, flamingos$species), margin = 1)

        #&gt;               Greater Flamingo  Lesser Flamingo
        #&gt;   Bogoria                0.081            0.919
        #&gt;   Elementaita            0.441            0.559
        #&gt;   Nakuru                 0.171            0.829
      </program>

      <p>
        Read this way, Bogoria is overwhelmingly lesser flamingo (92%), while
        Elementaita is close to an even split (44% greater, 56% lesser).
        Species composition clearly varies by lake. Neither the raw counts
        nor the row proportions are <q>more correct</q> than the other <mdash
        /> they answer different questions, and it is worth deciding which
        question you actually mean before you compute either one.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-lake-ecology">
      <title>Why might this be?</title>

      <p>
        Before reading the explanation, discuss with a neighbour: what could
        explain why lesser flamingos so heavily dominate Bogoria specifically,
        while Elementaita is close to an even split? Think about what each
        species eats.
      </p>

      <remark xml:id="remark-lake-ecology">
        <title>A possible ecological explanation</title>
        <p>
          Lake Bogoria's extreme alkalinity supports massive blooms of the
          microscopic cyanobacteria that lesser flamingos are specially
          adapted to filter-feed on. Greater flamingos rely more on
          invertebrates in the sediment, food that is less concentrated at
          Bogoria's most alkaline waters, which is consistent with their
          stronger presence at Elementaita.
        </p>
        <p>
          Notice what kind of claim this is: a plausible story that fits the
          pattern, not something the pivot table itself proves. The table
          tells you <em>that</em> composition differs by lake; it takes
          outside knowledge of flamingo biology to say <em>why</em>.
        </p>
      </remark>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-exploring-data">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-explore-bill-length">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Before computing anything, predict: do you expect
          <c>bill_length_mm</c> to look like a single hump, or two, once
          plotted? Now load the data, plot a histogram of
          <c>bill_length_mm</c>, and check. If you see more than one hump,
          try colouring or splitting by <c>species</c> and see whether that
          explains it, the way splitting by species explained the body mass
          pattern in <xref ref="ss-splitting-by-species" />.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-explore-lake-sex">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Build a pivot table of <c>lake</c> against <c>sex</c>, the same way
          <xref ref="ss-a-simple-pivot-table" /> built one for <c>lake</c>
          against <c>species</c>. Does the balance of males and females look
          different from lake to lake? Say which comparison <mdash /> row
          proportions or column proportions <mdash /> you used to answer that,
          and why you chose it.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-explore-write-up">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Pick any one numeric variable in the dataset other than
          <c>body_mass_g</c> or <c>bill_length_mm</c>. Look at it <mdash />
          however you like: a histogram, a table of rounded values, sorting
          it and scanning by eye. Write two or three sentences describing
          what you notice and one question it raises that you cannot yet
          answer. There is no single correct answer here; the point is
          practising the habit of looking before summarising.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>

</chapter>
